### Tested versions

v4.5.1.stable.mono.official [f62fdbde1] on Linux (Also tested on beta 3 of 4.6)





### System information

Godot v4.5.1.stable.mono - Fedora Linux 43 (Workstation Edition) on Wayland - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - integrated AMD Radeon 890M Graphics (RADV GFX1150) - AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX 370 w/ Radeon 890M (24 threads) - 30.45 GiB memory

### Issue description

If you connect to PropertyListChanged signal on a node tool script in C#, it'll end up calling that function on rebuild within the editor, but the object is already disposed.

It results in a stacktrace and a "Failed to unload assemblies" issue.

### Steps to reproduce

The minimal project below is simple enough to see the steps. But essentially you just need a script in this form:

```
using Godot;

[Tool]
public partial class TestNode : AnimatedSprite2D
{
    public override void _Ready()
    {
        PropertyListChanged += OnPropertyListChanged;
    }

    private void OnPropertyListChanged()
    {
        if (!IsInstanceValid(this))
        {
            GD.Print("TestNode is not valid");
        }

        GD.Print($"Sprite frames: {SpriteFrames}");
    }
}
```

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

https://github.com/hworld/godot-property-list-changed-dispose

Just change the csharp script to make sure a rebuild actually does anything (add a new line or something) and rebuild. You should see that the signal was called even though the node is disposed.

I wasn't able to trigger this to happen within a gdscript script.
